tcpcat (1.10.0-0kali1) kali-dev; urgency=medium

  * New upstream release 1.10.0.
    - tcpcat's offline AI: --ai says what to fix, why and how; --ai-deep
      verifies its findings with read-only probes, tells how the scan went
      and what blocked it, and plans the fixes.
    - --top-ports uses tcpcat's own list of 1,361 ports, -p- scans every
      port, and the default --rate is 1000.
    - CVE lookups use the distribution's own fix status.
    - --help prints a short help; --help-all lists every option. The
      IDS/IPS evasion options are deprecated and print a warning.
  * debian/control, debian/tcpcat.1: say what tcpcat is for, and document
    the AI options and the "tcpcat ai" subcommand.

 -- Nicolas Blondelle <support@tcpcat.io>  Fri, 27 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0100

tcpcat (1.7.0-0kali1) kali-dev; urgency=medium

  * New upstream release 1.7.0.
    - Raw-socket scans use 5 to 8 times less CPU per probe.
    - --ebpf no longer reports unsent probes as filtered under load, no
      longer crashes when a send fails, and uses the raw-socket engine in
      AF_XDP copy mode (--ebpf-copy keeps AF_XDP).
  * debian/control: describe exposure analysis, policy checks, evidence and
    passive discovery.
  * debian/tcpcat.1: document --ebpf-copy and the copy-mode fallback.

 -- Nicolas Blondelle <support@tcpcat.io>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 19:00:00 +0100

